<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
      Asymmetric exit bracket: cap the stop-loss, trail the profit.

      Two separate problems, one changeset, because they are the two halves of
      the same decision - let the target run, do not let the stop.

      1. max_sl_points - the cap
      ==========================
      sl_pct (027) scales the stop to the entry premium, which is what makes one
      config work across the 80-250 band. But it scales in both directions: at
      the top of the band 0.30 of a 250-point leg is a 75-point stop, and a
      75-point adverse move on one lot of 75 is 5,625 rupees on a trade whose
      target is 50. The percentage is right about *shape* and wrong about
      *absolute exposure*, because rupee risk is not a function of the premium
      the leg happened to open at.

      So the resolved stop is now min(resolved, max_sl_points). Whichever is
      lower wins. Worked through the standing band with sl_pct = 0.30:

        entry premium    0.30 of entry    capped stop    which applied
              80             24               24          percentage
             150             45               45          percentage
             200             60               60          equal
             250             75               60          cap

      Only the top of the band is touched, which is the intent: the cap is a
      ceiling on loss, not a second stop rule competing with the first.

      No cap on the target side. A target that resolves high is a target on a
      leg that has the room to travel; the payoff asymmetry that justifies
      capping the loss (a short option's gain is bounded by the premium, its
      loss is not) does not argue for bounding the gain as well.

      2. trail_ladder - the ratchet
      =============================
      A fixed bracket exits at the same two prices it was born with, so a trade
      that runs +55 and then reverses gives all of it back and closes at the
      original stop. The ladder converts realised excursion into a floor:

        peak profit reaches    stop moves to
             25 pts               +2   (trade can no longer lose)
             50 pts              +25
             75 pts              +50
            100 pts              +75

      Read as "trigger:lock", both in premium points, ascending, comma
      separated - the same one-column-one-owner encoding as strategy_ids (035),
      parsed only by com.moneymaker.util.TrailLadder. Stored verbatim rather
      than as step/giveback arithmetic so the rungs can be uneven: tightening
      faster at the top of a move is a config edit, not a code change.

      +2 rather than +0 on the first rung: a scratch exit still pays two legs of
      brokerage, so a stop at breakeven closes at a net loss. 2 points on 75
      units covers it.

      Latched off trade_order.peak_profit, so the ladder is a ratchet - once
      +50 is touched the +25 floor stays even if price falls back to +30. The
      stop only ever tightens.

      Ladder exits are recorded as exit_reason = TRAIL_SL, not STOP_LOSS. They
      are the opposite outcome - a TRAIL_SL closes green, a STOP_LOSS closes red
      - and folding them together would make the ledger's stop-loss rate
      unreadable exactly when the ladder starts working.

      NULL / blank ladder = no trailing, i.e. pre-036 behaviour. Existing rows
      are seeded with the ladder above rather than left null: this is the
      bracket the configs are meant to trade from now on, and a null would
      silently keep them on the fixed stop this changeset exists to replace.

      Interaction worth knowing
      =========================
      The first rung must be reachable before the target or the ladder never
      arms. With target_pct = 0.20, an 80-point leg targets 16 - below the
      25-point first rung - so the ladder is inert on the cheap end of the band
      and only starts mattering above a ~125-point entry. That is not a bug to
      fix here; it is the reason the rungs are per-config data.
    -->
    <changeSet id="036_add_trailing_sl_to_trade_config" author="moneymaker">
        <addColumn tableName="trade_config">
            <column name="max_sl_points" type="DECIMAL(12,4)" defaultValueNumeric="60"/>
            <column name="trail_ladder" type="VARCHAR(128)" defaultValue="25:2,50:25,75:50,100:75"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      addColumn's default covers rows inserted from here on; MySQL also
      backfills existing rows with it. The explicit updates make that guarantee
      independent of the DB engine's addColumn semantics, and are scoped to NULL
      so a re-run cannot overwrite a ladder someone has since edited.
    -->
    <changeSet id="036_seed_trailing_sl_defaults" author="moneymaker">
        <update tableName="trade_config">
            <column name="max_sl_points" valueNumeric="60"/>
            <where>max_sl_points IS NULL</where>
        </update>
        <update tableName="trade_config">
            <column name="trail_ladder" value="25:2,50:25,75:50,100:75"/>
            <where>trail_ladder IS NULL</where>
        </update>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      The same two columns on the rule table the EOD detector generates configs
      from, NOT NULL with defaults for the reason 026/027 gave: Hibernate writes
      every column explicitly, so a generated config never picks up trade_config's
      DB default. A null here would quietly hand every AUTO_DOWNTREND config an
      uncapped stop and no ladder - i.e. exactly the behaviour this changeset
      exists to replace, on the configs nobody hand-checks.
    -->
    <changeSet id="036_add_trailing_sl_to_sma_downtrend_rule" author="moneymaker">
        <addColumn tableName="sma_downtrend_rule">
            <column name="max_sl_points" type="DECIMAL(12,4)" defaultValueNumeric="60">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="trail_ladder" type="VARCHAR(128)" defaultValue="25:2,50:25,75:50,100:75">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Per-trade snapshot, for the same reason target_at_entry / stop_loss_at_entry
      are snapshotted (011): PositionService reads the order row, never the live
      config, so editing a ladder at 13:00 cannot retroactively re-floor a trade
      that opened at 09:20.

      trail_sl_at is the currently latched floor in signed premium points -
      positive is locked profit. NULL means no rung has been reached yet, which
      is also what every pre-036 row reads as.
    -->
    <changeSet id="036_add_trail_columns_to_trade_order" author="moneymaker">
        <addColumn tableName="trade_order">
            <column name="trail_ladder_at_entry" type="VARCHAR(128)"/>
            <column name="trail_sl_at" type="DECIMAL(12,4)"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
